WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.831
Cryptographic design starts with a
security property and a trust boundary.

2
00:00:04.831 --> 00:00:08.031
Confidentiality prevents unauthorized
parties from learning protected data.

3
00:00:08.031 --> 00:00:09.726
Integrity detects unauthorized changes.

4
00:00:09.726 --> 00:00:12.739
Authenticity supplies evidence that a
message or protected

5
00:00:12.739 --> 00:00:16.316
value was produced by someone possessing
the appropriate key.

6
00:00:16.316 --> 00:00:18.670
These properties overlap in common
constructions,

7
00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:20.741
but they are not interchangeable.

8
00:00:20.741 --> 00:00:24.601
Identify who may read or modify a value,
where it

9
00:00:24.601 --> 00:00:28.272
travels, and which components must be
trusted with plaintext.

10
00:00:28.272 --> 00:00:30.343
Different goals require different
primitives.

11
00:00:30.343 --> 00:00:32.603
Encryption supports recovery of
confidential data

12
00:00:32.603 --> 00:00:34.674
by an authorized key holder.

13
00:00:34.674 --> 00:00:36.745
Authenticated encryption also detects
tampering.

14
00:00:36.745 --> 00:00:40.511
A message authentication code protects
integrity and authenticity between parties

15
00:00:40.511 --> 00:00:44.182
sharing a secret, but does not encrypt the
message.

16
00:00:44.182 --> 00:00:47.195
Digital signatures use a private signing
key and

17
00:00:47.195 --> 00:00:50.302
public verification key; the verifier must
establish that

18
00:00:50.302 --> 00:00:53.502
the public key belongs to the expected
identity.

19
00:00:53.502 --> 00:00:56.515
Password verification instead needs a
dedicated password hash

20
00:00:56.515 --> 00:01:00.092
so the application need not recover the
original password.

21
00:01:00.092 --> 00:01:02.164
Representation is another distinct
concern.

22
00:01:02.164 --> 00:01:04.423
Base64 and hexadecimal turn bytes into

23
00:01:04.423 --> 00:01:06.494
convenient text without adding
confidentiality.

24
00:01:06.494 --> 00:01:09.695
Ordinary hashing produces a digest, not
encrypted data,

25
00:01:09.695 --> 00:01:12.519
and does not make predictable input
confidential.

26
00:01:12.519 --> 00:01:16.473
A fast hash by itself is unsuitable for
password storage.

27
00:01:16.473 --> 00:01:19.486
Reversibly encrypting login passwords also
gives the application

28
00:01:19.486 --> 00:01:22.687
a recovery capability it normally should
not have.

29
00:01:22.687 --> 00:01:26.076
Selecting a primitive by its convenient
output shape can

30
00:01:26.076 --> 00:01:29.653
therefore produce a system that solves the
wrong problem.

31
00:01:29.653 --> 00:01:32.572
Use mature, supported constructions
through reviewed interfaces.

32
00:01:32.572 --> 00:01:35.396
Avoid designing a cipher, padding scheme,
or

33
00:01:35.396 --> 00:01:38.220
improvised combination of encryption and
message authentication.

34
00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:40.856
Prefer a high level authenticated API that

35
00:01:40.856 --> 00:01:43.869
centralizes parameters, key selection, and
failure behavior.

36
00:01:43.869 --> 00:01:46.882
Application encryption still needs access
control and transport

37
00:01:46.882 --> 00:01:50.553
protection: use TLS even when selected
fields are encrypted.

38
00:01:50.553 --> 00:01:54.507
Minimize what must be stored and which
components require decryption.

39
00:01:54.507 --> 00:01:58.649
This keeps the security goal connected to
architecture instead of reducing

40
00:01:58.649 --> 00:02:02.980
cryptography to an isolated call that
happens to return unreadable bytes.

